---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import Breadcrumbs from '../components/Breadcrumbs.astro';
import PlanningCTA from '../components/PlanningCTA.astro';
import { images } from '../data/assets';
const breadcrumbs=[{label:'Support & What’s Included',href:'/support/'}];
---
<BaseLayout title="Group Trip Support & What’s Included | Talon Expeditions" description="Understand how Talon Expeditions supports group trip planning, organizer coordination, travel logistics, traveler preparation, and on-trip operations, and which items vary by itinerary." image={images.packing} breadcrumbs={breadcrumbs}>
  <section class="page-hero" style={`background-image:url('${images.packing}')`}><div class="shell"><Breadcrumbs items={breadcrumbs}/><p class="eyebrow">Support & what’s included</p><h1 class="section-title">“Fully supported” should explain the work, not hide it.</h1><p>Every Talon itinerary should make it clear what we are planning, what suppliers are delivering, what the traveler is responsible for, and which services depend on the specific destination or trip type.</p></div></section>
  <section class="section"><div class="shell"><div class="answer-panel"><h2>What does “fully supported group travel” mean?</h2><p>It means the core planning and travel dependencies are coordinated as one trip rather than leaving the organizer and travelers to assemble them independently. That can include itinerary design, accommodation, transport, activities, organizer support, participant communication, readiness, and on-trip coordination. It does not mean every possible expense is included or that travel can never change.</p></div><div class="specialty-heading" style="margin-top:50px"><div><p class="eyebrow">Support by stage</p><h2 class="section-title">What Talon can do before, during, and around the trip.</h2></div><p class="section-copy">The exact scope is confirmed in the proposal and trip documents. The categories below describe the support architecture, not a promise that every item is included on every journey.</p></div><div class="donor-grid"><div class="donor-card"><span class="card-index">01</span><h3>Plan</h3><p>Group discovery, destination comparison, itinerary design, route and pace, accommodation, transport, activity structure, and support model.</p></div><div class="donor-card"><span class="card-index">02</span><h3>Coordinate</h3><p>Supplier relationships, rooming assumptions, transfers, local operators, guides or instructors, timing, dependencies, and practical traveler information.</p></div><div class="donor-card"><span class="card-index">03</span><h3>Prepare</h3><p>Documents, insurance requirements, fitness, equipment, certification, packing, arrival details, participant questions, and final itinerary information.</p></div><div class="donor-card"><span class="card-index">04</span><h3>Support</h3><p>Defined on-trip support contacts, local supplier coordination, host or guide roles, and assistance within the agreed scope when plans change.</p></div><div class="donor-card"><span class="card-index">05</span><h3>Extend</h3><p>Pre-trip or post-trip nights, independent extensions, additional local travel, or related arrangements where practical.</p></div><div class="donor-card"><span class="card-index">06</span><h3>Learn</h3><p>After-trip feedback, organizer lessons, repeat-program improvement, and a clearer starting point for the next group departure.</p></div></div></div></section>
  <section class="section dark-section"><div class="shell"><div class="specialty-heading"><div><p class="eyebrow">What varies</p><h2 class="section-title">Some support is trip-specific by definition.</h2></div><p class="section-copy" style="color:#aaa">A scuba program, cruise group, cultural itinerary, and mountain trek need different suppliers and operating boundaries. We should not pretend otherwise.</p></div><div class="why-grid" style="border-color:#302a26"><div class="why-item"><span class="number">01</span><h3>Air travel</h3><p style="color:#aaa">May be arranged, coordinated, or booked independently depending on the group and itinerary.</p></div><div class="why-item"><span class="number">02</span><h3>Guides & instructors</h3><p style="color:#aaa">Used where appropriate or required and provided by qualified destination or activity specialists.</p></div><div class="why-item"><span class="number">03</span><h3>Meals & incidentals</h3><p style="color:#aaa">Some trips include selected meals; others intentionally leave travelers more local choice.</p></div><div class="why-item"><span class="number">04</span><h3>On-trip hosting</h3><p style="color:#aaa">May be Talon-led, supplier-led, organizer-led, locally guided, or a hybrid depending on the journey.</p></div></div></div></section>
  <section class="section"><div class="shell definition-grid"><div class="definition-sticky"><p class="eyebrow">Traveler responsibilities</p><h2>Support does not remove personal responsibility.</h2></div><div class="definition-body"><p>Travelers remain responsible for maintaining valid passports and visas, providing accurate personal information, meeting medical and activity prerequisites, following provider instructions, arriving appropriately prepared, and obtaining required or recommended insurance.</p><p>Weather, airline operations, border rules, supplier changes, medical events, and local conditions can also affect travel. No credible operator should imply that a “supported” trip eliminates those external realities. The value of support is clearer planning, communication, and assistance within the agreed scope when the real world changes.</p><div class="two-col-list"><div class="list-card">Passport validity and entry eligibility</div><div class="list-card">Personal medical decisions and medications</div><div class="list-card">Required insurance and policy suitability</div><div class="list-card">Fitness and honest activity self-assessment</div><div class="list-card">Personal equipment and packing</div><div class="list-card">Following final trip and supplier instructions</div></div></div></div></section>
  <PlanningCTA title="Tell us how much support the organizer actually wants to carry." />
</BaseLayout>
